# Localization and UX rules

Vimla supports at least Russian (`ru`) and English (`en`).

## Strings
- No user-facing hard-coded strings in components, form validators, notifications or email/SMS templates.
- Use centralized translation keys/dictionaries.
- Translation keys must describe meaning, not copy text (`auth.signIn`, not `sign_in_text_1`).
- Missing translations must be detectable in development/tests.

## Errors
- Backend/domain returns stable machine-readable error codes.
- Frontend maps codes to localized human-readable text.
- Do not expose raw provider/DB/internal English errors to users.
- Field-level errors appear next to fields; form-level errors appear in the form. Do not rely on browser validation popovers.
- Validation must remain server-enforced even when frontend validates first.

## Locale-sensitive formatting
- Use locale-aware formatting for dates, times, pluralization, numbers and currencies.
- Store timestamps in UTC; localize only at UI/email presentation.
- Preserve locale preference in a user preference/session-compatible source, with browser locale fallback.

## Product flows
- New UI flows must be usable in both RU and EN before they are considered complete.
- Auth emails/SMS and security notifications must use the recipient's locale when known.
